ANNOTATED CODE FOR DESTROY ARMOR

This part of code is in file read.c starting from line 783.

1: case SCR_DESTROY_ARMOR:
2:    {

Start of the code for destroy armor handling.

3:    otmp = some_armor(&youmonst);

 From this point on otmp holds a pointer to some armor the the character 
was wearing if any.

4:    if(confused) {

Reading the scroll when confused is handled here.

5:       if(!otmp) {

If an armor piece was not found do the following.

6:          strange_feeling(sobj,"Your bones itch.");

strange_feeling() is a function that either prints the given text or 
just a strange feeling text based on the characters experience. It also 
let's you give the object a name if the object is unidentified and 
unnamed. Lastly it removes the object from the memory with all 
references to it and frees the memory.

7:          exercise(A_STR, FALSE);
8:          exercise(A_CON, FALSE);

Abuse strength and constitution.

9:          return(1);

Let the calling function know that the scroll is already used up.

10:      }

Now we handle the armor.

11:      otmp->oerodeproof = sobj->cursed;

Fooproof the armor if the scroll is cursed. Notice that the possible 
damage the armor piece has had is not cleared. sobj is a pointer to the 
scroll.

12:      p_glow2(otmp, NH_PURPLE);

Show a glow message.

13:      break;
14:   }

End of confused handling.

15:   if(!sobj->cursed || !otmp || !otmp->cursed) {

The following is done if no armor was found or both the scroll and the 
armor is non-cursed.

16:      if(!destroy_arm(otmp)) {

Destroys the armor. destroy_arm() function (described below) returns 
true if the armor pointed by otmp was destroyed. If the destruction 
failed (the armor resisted succesfully) or no armor was worn then the 
following happens.

17:         strange_feeling(sobj,"Your skin itches.");
18:         exercise(A_STR, FALSE);
19:         exercise(A_CON, FALSE);
20:         return(1);

So just like in confused reading: give a strange feeling message (albeit 
a little bit different, check line 6), abuse strength and constitution 
and tell the calling function that the scroll is already used up.

21:      } else
22:         known = TRUE;

If an armor piece was destroyed then the scroll is identified.

23:   } else {	/* armor and scroll both cursed */
24:      Your("%s %s.", xname(otmp), otense(otmp, "vibrate"));

If both the armor and scroll is cursed give a message.

25:      if (otmp->spe >= -6) otmp->spe--;

Decrease the enchantment of the armor by 1 but only if the enchantment 
level is higher than -7. This eliminates the underflow abuse of destroy 
armor.

26:      make_stunned(HStun + rn1(10, 10), TRUE);

Make the character 10..19 turns more stunned.

rn1(x,y) returns a random value in the range y..y+x-1.

27:   }
28: }
29: break;

This ends the handling in the normal case. The default return value (not 
shown here) tells the calling function that the scroll is not used up.

Also unless the variable known was set to true there is chance to give 
name to the scroll unless it is already named.


This part of code is in file do_wear.c starting from line 2102.

1: /* hit by destroy armor scroll/black dragon breath/monster spell */
2: int
3: destroy_arm(atmp)

Here is the function destroy_arm() and it starts with a function header 
that tells the return value (here integer) and the parameters. The 
return value tells if the armor pointed by atmp is destroyed: 0=no, 
non-zero=yes.

4: register struct obj *atmp;

The parameter is defined as a pointer to an object.

5: {

This starts the function body.

6:    register struct obj *otmp;

Declaration of an additional pointer to an object needed by the 
following macro.

7: #define DESTROY_ARM(o) ((otmp = (o)) != 0 && \
8:                        (!atmp || atmp == otmp) && \
9:                        (!obj_resists(otmp, 0, 90)))
10:

Here is a macro defintion with a macro parameter o. In macro-invocation 
the formal parameter is substituted into the macros text and the macro 
invocation is replaced with the resulting expanded text. The 
'\'-character on the end of lines 7 and 8 tells that the text continues 
on the next line.

So from that definition of the macro with an invocation like 
DESTROY_ARM(uarmc) we get the following expansion (uramc is the pointer 
to the worn cloak):

((otmp = (uarmc)) != 0 && (!atmp || atmp == otmp) && (!obj_resists(otmp, 
0, 90)))

The first test is 'are the character wearing a cloak?' If this fails the 
expression is false and rest of the expression is not evaluated, 
otherwise otmp holds now a pointer to the cloak.

The second test is 'is it true that no armor is to be destroyed or is 
the armor to be destroyed same as pointed by otmp (uarmc)?' If this 
fails (armor to be destroyed is different than uarmc) the expression is 
false and the rest of the expression is not evaluated, otherwise we 
continue to the third test.

The third test is negation of 'does the armor resist?' The function 
obj_resists() (described below) returns true if the object resists. The 
parameters passed here is the pointer to the object, 0% chance for 
ordinary objects, 90% chance for artifacts.

So in short the macro DESTROY_ARM() returns true if there is an armor 
piece to be destroyed and it is the one that we test against and it 
didn't resisist the destruction or if there is not a specific armor 
piece to destroy then true if the tested piece does not resist.

The effect of this is that when there is no definite piece of armor to 
destroy the order is: cloak, body armor, undergarment, headgear, 
handgear, footwear, shield. As the only artifact armor is The Mitre of 
Holiness the order is fixed up until headgear which might resist.


11:   if (DESTROY_ARM(uarmc)) {

First we test if the selected piece was the cloak. If true do the following:

12:      if (donning(otmp)) cancel_don();

If you are in the middle of wearing the cloak cancel the wearing action.

13:      Your("%s crumbles and turns to dust!",
14:         cloak_simple_name(uarmc));

Give a message.

15:      (void) Cloak_off();

Remove the cloak. This handles all the necessary adjustment on 
displacing, invisibility etc.

16:      useup(otmp);

This removes the piece of armor from existense so that it does not 
appear on any internal structures that nethack uses to keep track of the 
objects and releases all the memory that was used to store the object 
information.

Notice here that the variable otmp is used. This could have been written 
using uarmc as the pointer as we know that these two point to the same 
object. But this doesn't matter useup() handles all the details. For 
example here useup() has to clear uarmc so that it no longer points to 
the destroyed cloak.

17:   else if (DESTROY_ARM(uarm)) {
18:      if (donning(otmp)) cancel_don();
19:      Your("armor turns to dust and falls to the %s!",
20:         surface(u.ux,u.uy));
21:      (void) Armor_gone();
22:      useup(otmp);

Ditto for the body armor.

23: #ifdef TOURIST

This start conditional compilation. The following part of the code is 
compiled in only if TOURIST is set.

24:   } else if (DESTROY_ARM(uarmu)) {
25:      if (donning(otmp)) cancel_don();
26:      Your("shirt crumbles into tiny threads and falls apart!");
27:      (void) Shirt_off();
28:      useup(otmp);

Ditto for the undergarment.

29: #endif

This ends the conditional compilation.

30:   } else if (DESTROY_ARM(uarmh)) {
31:      if (donning(otmp)) cancel_don();
32:      Your("helmet turns to dust and is blown away!");
33:      (void) Helmet_off();
34:      useup(otmp);

Ditto for the headgear.

35:   } else if (DESTROY_ARM(uarmg)) {
36:      if (donning(otmp)) cancel_don();
37:      Your("gloves vanish!");
38:      (void) Gloves_off();
39:      useup(otmp);

Ditto for the handgear.

40:      selftouch("You");

Special addition to the handgear. If the characters handgear disappears 
then whatever is wielded touches the hands. This might lead to 
petrification.

41:   } else if (DESTROY_ARM(uarmf)) {
42:      if (donning(otmp)) cancel_don();
43:      Your("boots disintegrate!");
44:      (void) Boots_off();
45:      useup(otmp);

Ditto for the footwear.

46:   } else if (DESTROY_ARM(uarms)) {
47:      if (donning(otmp)) cancel_don();
48:      Your("shield crumbles away!");
49:      (void) Shield_off();
50:      useup(otmp);

Last but not least: the shield.

51:   } else {
52:      return 0;		/* could not destroy anything */
53:   }
54:

Like the comment says if we get to this else-branch nothing was 
destroyed and we notify the caller by returning failure.

55: #undef DESTROY_ARM

The macro DESTROY_ARM is undefined as it is unnecessary after this 
point. Notice how much readable the code gets when one defines a complex 
macro like that to do a repetitive job.

56:   stop_occupation();

If an armor piece was destroyed stop any occupation that the character 
might be in.

57:   return(1);

Tell the caller that a destruction was succesful.

58: }

End of the function definition.


