1: boolean
2: obj_resists(obj, ochance, achance)

Here is the function obj_resists() and it starts with a function header 
that tells the return value (here boolean) and the parameters. The 
return value tells if the object pointed by obj resits or not.

3: struct obj *obj;
4: int ochance, achance;	/* percent chance for ordinary objects, 
artifacts */

The parameters are defined.

obj as a pointer to an object.
ochance and achance as integers.

5: {

This starts the function body.

6:   if (obj->otyp == AMULET_OF_YENDOR ||
7:      obj->otyp == SPE_BOOK_OF_THE_DEAD ||
8:      obj->otyp == CANDELABRUM_OF_INVOCATION ||
9:      obj->otyp == BELL_OF_OPENING ||
10:     (obj->otyp == CORPSE && is_rider(&mons[obj->corpsenm]))) {
11:        return TRUE;

First we check for the amulet, the invocation artifacts and rider 
corpses. These are indestructible so they resist everything.

12:   } else {
13:      int chance = rn2(100);

Otherwise we declare a new integer variable chance that is initialized 
with a random value from the range 0..99.

14:      return((boolean)(chance < (obj->oartifact ? achance : ochance)));
15:   }

The return value is the truth value of the less than comparison. 
Artifacts uses achance other objects ochance in the comparison.

